# "postulant boils from The souls of his feet" -> "pustulant boils from the soles of his feet" (16849, Job 2:7).

=== Introduction
--- Job 1–2 [title]
# image: lessons/job/job-1-2-the-trial-in-heaven.jpg (shadow-play title art, to be added)
overline: The Job class · Part 1 of 5
heading: Job 1–2: The Trial Begins
sub: The greatest man on Earth at this moment, according to God.
refs: Job 1:1; Job 1:21; Job 2:10
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- Where we are
heading: Where we are: Part 1, Job 1–2
tile: 1–2 | The trial begins
tile: 3–31 | Job and his three friends
tile: 32–37 | Elihu speaks
tile: 38–41 | Out of the whirlwind
tile: 42 | The latter end of Job
p: If the Book of Job was one chapter, the lesson is present and clear. But as we'll see, there is so many more lessons to learn than simple perseverance in faith.
take: The Book of Job is the finest example of literature ever written.

=== A man in the land of Uz
# from 16841
--- There was a man in the land of Uz
kicker: Job 1
scripture: Job 1:1-3 | mark: that man was perfect and upright, and one that feared God, and eschewed evil
p: The Book of Job begins with the introduction of a man in the land of Uz, the titular character; Job. Uz, from the biblical clues appears to be a fertile plain in the Arabian peninsula, near where Edom would be. The time frame, many believe, is during the era of the patriarchs; Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.

# from 16841
--- Before Abraham's seed
kicker: Job 1
p: It would be good to note that, Abraham, or Abram, was a Chaldean, a nation who are mentioned here in this chapter located in what is modern Iraq. Where the seat of Babylonian power would be. And he was called out from the city of Ur there, to go unto Hebron by God.
p: And, as mentioned throughout his story and his sons', there were people in this post flood era who feared the Lord, and offered sacrifices unto him.
p: Of course we know about Melchizedek, King of Salem, and high priest, and the tenth of the spoils offered by Abram to Melchizedek. Many consider him to be a pre-incarnate appearance of Jesus.
callout: All this to say that the idea of the worship of God originating with Abraham, and only his seed after the flesh, is incorrect. There were many following the flood and the dispersion of nations out from Babel, that called upon the name of the Lord for salvation, and sought to offer the blood as a picture of their faith in Him, as we see Job does for himself and his family.

# from 16841
--- Salvation always offered to all people
kicker: Job 1
p: One of the conceits of modern Zionism and dispensationalism holds that only the sons of Israel after the flesh were special, and that they invented monotheism. But throughout the Bible we find people who were not of that line, and not given to idols and false gods. It's important to understand that salvation has always been by grace alone through faith in the Lord, calling upon his name. And that salvation was always offered to all people.
p: We will see in Exodus the mixed multitudes that followed Israel into the wilderness from Egypt. We will see high priests of other nations that fear the Lord. There will be people like Ruth and Rahab and others who have the faith.
p: The distinction for Israel was that they were given the oracles of God. That they had the prophets and the word of God delivered directly to them, and given the law of the Lord that they may be perfect, complete, and a light unto the Gentiles. The other nations. Calling all men to repentance.

# from 16841, 16842
--- Thus did Job continually
kicker: Job 1
scripture: Job 1:4-5 | mark: Thus did Job continually
p: But we see the greatest man on Earth at this moment, according to God, who feared Him, eschewing evil, offering sacrifices to sanctify himself and his family. Without the law. Without the temple. Without a priesthood. Without the oracles of God, knowing by the faith of his fathers what was expected.
p: And so we find Job, faithfully keeping the sanctification of sacrifices for himself and his children, and he has been blessed beyond measure by God.
p: 10 children, seven sons and three daughters. Incredible wealth and cattle and livestock. Great land and a great house. And all his adult children successful in that they were established in their own homes to have their own feasts. Having established all of this, the Bible shifts focus to the throne of God in Heaven.

=== Before the Lord
# from 16842
--- The sons of God came
kicker: Job 1
big: Job 1:6 | mark: the sons of God came to present themselves before the LORD; Satan came also among them
p: I've covered this multiple times in other studies, but the sons of God here refers to the spirits of those saved by faith in Christ. The same as the sons of God in [[Genesis 6:2|Genesis 6]] being the children of obedience, through Seth. Because the only way to become A son of God, is by faith in THE Son of God.
p: So, not to belabor the point, but at no time has God called angels His sons. And further evidence of this is in the text itself, in verse 6. Satan came also among them.
p: Satan is an angel. A rebellious angel, a cherub. Perhaps a prominent angel but, an angel nonetheless. And he came among them, as a separate entity than them. Not as one of them.

# from 15859
--- Believers in heaven, not angels
kicker: The sons of God
big: Hebrews 1:5 | mark: unto which of the angels said he at any time, Thou art my Son
p: They're in heaven, so they must be believers—only the redeemed go there ([[Hebrews 12:22-23]]). Satan's "among them" but not one of them; he's the accuser, not a son. The repetition in [[Job 2:1]] changes nothing—same believers, same contrast with Satan.
p: The Bible makes it clear how one becomes a "son of God": through faith in the Lord. This is the foundation—sonship is granted to humans who believe in Christ.
refs: John 1:12; Romans 8:14; Galatians 3:26
take: God is not the author of confusion. If your interpretation of scripture causes a contradiction, creates a question that cannot be answered by comparing scripture to scripture, then it's wrong.
read: app:doctrine-angels~15859 | The sons of God are believers, never angels
read: genesis-6-sons-of-god | Genesis 6: Sons of God

# from 16842
--- To and fro in the earth
kicker: Job 1
scripture: Job 1:7 | mark: From going to and fro in the earth, and from walking up and down in it
p: And God asks him where he's been, and he says going to and fro in the earth and walking up and down in it.
big: 1 Peter 5:8 | mark: your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about
p: Christians should remember that Satan continues this practice today. And like his appearance before God, to accuse Job, he does the same of us even now.

# from 16842
--- The accuser of our brethren
kicker: Job 1
scripture: Revelation 12:9-11 | mark: the accuser of our brethren is cast down, which accused them before our God day and night
p: Satan is our adversary, the god of this world, and our accuser before the Lord.
p: I sometimes wonder if he even bothers with the unsaved, beyond giving them power that they can afflict those who are saved. His resentment is in those sons of God, that God so loved us that He gave His only begotten Son for us, knowing that hell was created for him and the angels he deceived.

=== Hast thou considered my servant Job?
# from 16843, 17880
--- Hast thou considered my servant Job?
kicker: Job 1
scripture: Job 1:8-11 | mark: Hast thou considered my servant Job; Doth Job fear God for nought?
p: Now God knows who Satan is and what he does and His pointing out Job is deliberate, and in so many ways this is done for our admonition, upon whom the end of the world has come.
p: If a man as upright and perfect in his ways can be sorely tempted by the persecution of Satan against him, If the hedge of protection may be lifted, to show the glory of God for such a man as Job, how can we expect the small tragedies that befall us as believers to be any less to His glory?
p: And in our darkest moments, the voices of the scoffers cut like a knife. Satan uses them to attack our faith, as he sought to shake Job's.

# from 16843
--- Blessed or cursed
kicker: Job 1
p: Satan of course credits the hedge of protection God has placed around Job and his family and the blessings of the holdings that he's acquired which are gifts of God, as the only reason Job's faith is so strong. He says to God that if He put forth His hand now and touched all that he had, Job would curse Him to his face.
panel: Only the wicked prosper? | I have seen an error among people similar to this, that says only the wicked prosper. Job shows this isn't the case and that wealth in itself is not a bad thing, but that all we have is a gift of God.
panel-warn: The prosperity Gospel | Then there's the prosperity Gospel types, who falsely say that if you're not successful and you're not succeeding, that you're not right with God.
p: But here we humbly learn that whether we are blessed or cursed, all is to His glory. As Paul writes:
big: Philippians 4:12 | mark: both to abound and to suffer need
take: Whether you're in good times or bad times or the mix most of us find ourselves in, it's all to the glory of God. His grace is sufficient.

# from 16843
--- All that he hath is in thy power
kicker: Job 1
scripture: Job 1:12 | mark: all that he hath is in thy power; only upon himself put not forth thine hand
p: Now the Lord says to Satan, *behold all that he hath is in thy power* but bars him from doing harm to Job physically.
p: The prime interpretation of this is that God has granted Satan special permission to afflict Job, And that is a fair reading of the text, especially in light of the hedge of protection Satan says God has placed about Job. But, as I've mentioned, the Bible says that Satan is the god of this world, That he is the prince of the power of the air, ruler of this world. Not as God, but as having influence and power over the things of this Earth.
p: So is the Lord reminding Satan, or enabling him? It's difficult to say. But we should keep it in mind always.
big: Ephesians 6:12 | mark: against the rulers of the darkness of this world

=== In one day
# from 16843, 16844
--- In one day, he does his worst
kicker: Job 1
p: But regardless, Satan is given the power to affect all that Job has, if not his person. And in one day, he does his worst. First we see that his sons and his daughters were eating and drinking wine and in the eldest brother's house, this detail is given before the rest, creating tension in the reader.
step: The Sabeans | Then the messengers begin to arrive. One tells of the oxen and asses killed by marauding Sabeans, who are from the south, where modern Yemen is.
step: The fire of God | Then another arrives to say that the fire of God rained from Heaven on the sheep and the servants and consumed them.
step: The Chaldeans | Before the second messenger can finish, a third arrives and says the Chaldeans made out three bands and fell upon the camels and slaying the servants. Of course this is a raiding party from the north, striking simultaneously with those from the south.
step: A great wind | Finally as the third messenger is conveying his report, A fourth arrives and says that while Job's sons and daughters were eating and drinking a great wind came from the wilderness and smote the four corners of the house and it fell on them and they are all dead.
refs: Job 1:13-15; Job 1:16; Job 1:17; Job 1:18-19

# from 16843
--- Fire from heaven
kicker: Job 1
p: When God gave permission to Satan to do what he will, he gave him access to the fire of God that rains from Heaven.
scripture: Revelation 13:11-13 | mark: he maketh fire come down from heaven on the earth in the sight of men
p: The false prophet of the Antichrist will have this power and ability, And it will cause the unsaved to worship the beast and his image.

# from 16844
--- Surrounded by evil
kicker: Job 1
lead: Job was beset from the south, from above, from the north and from all four corners, surrounded by evil. And the devil allowed a sole survivor from each.
p: The coordination and power and influence that the devil has where he can have two different armies attack at the same time, controlling fire from heaven, and the winds of the air, that the sole survivors of each would arrive one after the other, to absolutely devastate Job, shows Satan's cunning wickedness. Each blow would be sufficient to cripple most men. To have them come one after the other after the other after the other, in quick succession is unimaginable.
refs: Job 1:15; Job 1:16; Job 1:17; Job 1:19

# from 16844
--- And worshipped
kicker: Job 1
scripture: Job 1:20-22 | mark: fell down upon the ground, and worshipped; the LORD gave, and the LORD hath taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD; In all this Job sinned not, nor charged God foolishly
p: These were the manner and customs throughout the Old Testament for mourning. We see here that worship is defined as falling to your knees or bowing down. Job in absolute sorrow tears his clothes, shaves his head for the dead, and lays face first down upon the ground.
p: And the prayer Job makes contains two statements that have both become idioms even among the secular world.
take: And despite all of this tragedy, despite all of the cause Satan gave him to sin, Job sinned not. And he didn't blame God, but rather blessed His name.

# from 16846
--- Naked shall I return thither
kicker: Job 1
p: “Naked came I… naked shall I return thither” – “Thither” or shām in the Hebrew means, “to that place." Clearly Job doesn't mean the womb. The return is to what then? What is thither? The grave? "Sheol" and nothingness?
p: We're going to see that Job's faith is redemption through the Lord. Not that empty meaning that many give in their Old Testament interpretations.
p: Either way, Job sees life as lease, not ownership, that all is from God, Who is blessed forever. It's humbling and something we should always keep in mind. Life is a vapor.
big: Genesis 3:19 | mark: for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return

# from 16845
--- The finest example of literature
kicker: Job 1
lead: Thematically, as prose, this setup creates tension in the reader. I'll probably say this multiple times throughout this study, but the Book of Job is the finest example of literature ever written.
p: While its exact date of composition isn't known, it likely found its final form during the Babylonian captivity. Shortly thereafter, in the Hellenistic world, the Greek drama became a literary style. Acted out by people, to tell a story either from a monologue or a soliloquy or a troupe of performers.
p: Because we are gifted with the King James version, and its eloquent prose in what many would say is Shakespearean English, it's easy to picture the Book of Job as a play. And it is possible to perform, and many have. And it is greater than all of the plays the Bard penned.
take: Isn't it interesting that it was put to pen under the inspiration of God, separate from and before the Greek tragedies and comedies that the world now considers classic literature?

=== Skin for skin
# from 16849
--- Again before the Lord
kicker: Job 2
p: The Book of Job continues into chapter 2. I've often thought that, had Job been confined to just the first chapter it would serve as a great lesson in patience and understanding in the faith. But in the wisdom of God it continues, bringing many other lessons to us.
scripture: Job 2:1-3 | mark: still he holdeth fast his integrity, although thou movedst me against him, to destroy him without cause
p: Again, the spirits of the righteous men in Heaven come before God, and among them the devil comes. And again he's been to and fro in the Earth, and walking up and down it.
p: And the Lord said unto Satan hast thou considered Job? That there's none like him the perfect and upright man, who is still holding fast his integrity even though Satan had moved God to destroy him without a cause.

# from 16849
--- Touch his bone and his flesh
kicker: Job 2
scripture: Job 2:4-8 | mark: Skin for skin, yea, all that a man hath will he give for his life; but save his life
p: And Satan says that a man will give up anything for his life. He calls God to afflict Job by touching his bone and flesh and says that he will curse God to His face. And so God agrees, saying only take not his life. And Satan afflicts Job with pustulant boils from the soles of his feet to the crown of his head.
p: Poor Job sits in ash, mourning, And now is taking a shard of pottery to his skin to scrape himself of the sores. Miserable, having lost everything and now afflicted with a painful and disgusting curse.

# from 16849
--- Curse God, and die
kicker: Job 2
scripture: Job 2:9-10 | mark: curse God, and die; shall we receive good at the hand of God, and shall we not receive evil?; In all this did not Job sin with his lips
p: His wife is disgusted with his continued faith, and says curse God and die. But he said that this is foolish. That if we receive good at the hands of God shall we not receive evil?
p: And Job sinned not with his lips.
take: The continued patience of Job is a testament that we should seek to emulate. As bad as things are we've all been blessed beyond anything we deserve by God's grace. His grace is sufficient.

# from 16849
--- My grace is sufficient for thee
kicker: Job 2
p: Paul, similarly had bemoaned a thorn in his flesh, appealing to God three times saying that he would be so much more useful in the cause of Christ if he had it removed. It took him a while to understand.
scripture: 2 Corinthians 12:6-9 | mark: My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness
p: Our suffering exists for many reasons, but ultimately it is to give glory to God for His grace and His mercy. That while we are weak He is strong and our strength. Even if it's just to humble us that we glory not in ourselves.

# from 16849
--- Job's three friends
kicker: Job 2
scripture: Job 2:11-13 | mark: Eliphaz the Temanite, and Bildad the Shuhite, and Zophar the Naamathite; seven days and seven nights
panel: Eliphaz | the Temanite
panel: Bildad | the Shuhite
panel: Zophar | the Naamathite
p: And so the scene shifts as three of Job's friends arrive from far off, barely recognizing him. They enter and sit down with him on the ground, for 7 days and seven nights and none speak to him for they saw that his grief was very great.
take: We will learn more about each as the story progresses.

=== What we have seen
--- What we have seen
point: The greatest man on Earth | Without the law. Without the temple. Without a priesthood. Without the oracles of God, knowing by the faith of his fathers what was expected.
point: The sons of God | The only way to become A son of God, is by faith in THE Son of God.
point: The accuser | Satan is our adversary, the god of this world, and our accuser before the Lord.
point: Blessed or cursed | Whether we are blessed or cursed, all is to His glory. His grace is sufficient.
point: Job sinned not | Despite all of the cause Satan gave him to sin, Job sinned not. And he didn't blame God, but rather blessed His name.
